    "title": "Trump Announces 25% Tariffs on Auto Imports",
    "text": "• President Donald Trump announced a 25% tariff on auto imports on March 26, 2025.\n• The White House claims the tariffs will foster domestic manufacturing and raise $100 billion in annual revenue.\n• The tariffs will take effect in April 2025.\n• Trump stated that the tariffs are intended to spur growth and lead to more factories opening in the U.S.\n• Shares in General Motors fell approximately 3%, while Ford's stock increased slightly, and Stellantis shares dropped nearly 3.6% following the announcement.\n• Economist Mary Lovely warned that the tariffs could lead to higher vehicle prices, reduced choice, and more households being priced out of the new car market.\n• If fully passed onto consumers, the average auto price could increase by $12,500.\n• Trump proposed a tax deduction for interest paid on auto loans for vehicles made in America, which could reduce tariff revenue.\n• The auto tariffs are part of a broader strategy to impose reciprocal taxes on imports from other nations.\n• Trump has previously imposed a 20% import tax on all imports from China and 25% tariffs on Mexico and Canada.\n• The U.S. imported nearly 8 million cars and light trucks worth $244 billion last year, with Mexico, Japan, and South Korea as the top sources.\n• Imports of auto parts totaled over $197 billion, primarily from Mexico, Canada, and China.\n• The tariffs could risk igniting a global trade war, potentially impacting economic growth and raising prices for consumers.",
